meta {
  name: Operator - handoff inbox
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/operator/handoffs
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{operatorToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "tenantId": "self",
    "includeResolved": false
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function() {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("returns the conversations list", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().conversations).to.be.an("array");
  });
}

docs {
  Auth (in-Worker, enforced): send the operator app's bearer (`Authorization: Bearer
  <session token>` — verified against the cloud API's GET /me; the server-resolved tenantId must
  equal the request tenantId, else 403. Verified teammates use the owner tenantId; a legacy
  /me response without tenantId falls back to its nonempty user.id, and malformed identity
  fields fail closed) OR the server-to-server `x-tenant-sync-secret`. No/invalid
  credentials → 401. Verified bearers are cached in-isolate for 60s.

  The operator app's inbox: every pending or operator-owned session for the tenant, newest activity
  first, each with a `lastMessage` preview from the session's ring buffer. New handoffs use a
  Telegram-independent `handoff:<tenant>:<session>` KV index. The Worker unions its first page
  with the legacy session→thread index, de-duplicates ids, and asks each SessionDO for a one-shot
  summary. Telegram is optional, and later topic linking cannot overwrite discovery state.
  Missing `tenantId` → 400.
  Resolved sessions are EXCLUDED by default; pass `includeResolved: true` to list them
  too (rows carry `resolved: true`, `handoffState: "ai"` and `handedOff: false` — resolving
  hands the session back to the AI). `handedOff` is a compatibility boolean that is false
  while waiting and true after the operator's first reply.
  Response: `{ conversations: [{ sessionId, lastMessage, handoffState, handedOff, ts, resolved }] }`.
}
